CC   -!- FUNCTION: May play a role in the transport of aminophospholipids
CC       from the outer to the inner leaflet of various membranes and the
CC       maintenance of asymmetric distribution of phospholipids in the
CC       canicular membrane. May have a role in transport of bile acids
CC       into the canaliculus, uptake of bile acids from intestinal
CC       contents into intestinal mucosa or both.
CC   -!- CATALYTIC ACTIVITY: ATP + H(2)O = ADP + phosphate.
CC   -!- SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Integral membrane protein.
CC   -!- TISSUE SPECIFICITY: Found in most tissues except brain and
CC       skeletal muscle. Most abundant in pancreas and small intestine.
CC   -!- DISEASE: Defects in ATP8B1 are the cause of progressive familial
CC       intrahepatic cholestasis type 1 (PFIC1) [MIM:211600]; also known
CC       as Byler disease. PFIC1 is an autosomal recessive disorder,
CC       characterized by early infancy cholestasis, that may be initially
CC       episodic but progresses to malnutrition, growth retardation and
CC       end-stage liver disease before adulthood.
CC   -!- DISEASE: Defects in ATP8B1 are the cause of benign recurrent
CC       intrahepatic cholestasis (BRIC) [MIM:243300]; also known as
CC       Summerskill syndrome. BRIC is an autosomal recessive disorder,
CC       characterized by intermittent episodes of cholestasis without
CC       extrahepatic bile duct obstruction, with initial elevation of
CC       serum bile acids, followed by cholestatic jaundice which generally
CC       spontaneously resolves after periods of weeks to months.
CC   -!- DISEASE: Defects in ATP8B1 could be associated with recurrent
CC       intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (RICP) [MIM:147480]; also
CC       known as pregnancy-related cholestasis. RICP is characterized by
CC       generalized itching, with or without jaundice, absence of biliary
CC       colic, absence of jaundice or pruritus between pregnancies, and
CC       absence of chronic liver disease. Appears during pregnancy
CC       (primarily in the third trimester), and disappears shortly
CC       postpartum. May be a female-limited autosomal dominant
CC       inheritance.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Belongs to the cation transport ATPases family (P-type
CC       ATPases). Subfamily IV.
