CC   -!- FUNCTION: Catalyzes the deacetylation of N-acetylaspartic acid
CC       (NAA) to produce acetate and L-aspartate. NAA occurs in high
CC       concentration in brain and its hydrolysis NAA plays a significant
CC       part in the maintenance of intact white matter. In other tissues
CC       it act as a scavenger of NAA from body fluids.
CC   -!- CATALYTIC ACTIVITY: N-acyl-L-aspartate + H(2)O = a carboxylate +
CC       L-aspartate.
CC   -!- SUBUNIT: Homodimer (Probable).
CC   -!- TISSUE SPECIFICITY: Brain white matter, skeletal muscle, kidney,
CC       adrenal glands, lung and liver.
CC   -!- DISEASE: Defects in ASPA are the cause of Canavan disease (CD)
CC       [MIM:271900]; also known as spongy degeneration of the brain. CD
CC       is an autosomal recessive leukodystrophy clinically characterized
CC       by atonia of neck muscles, hyperextension of legs and flexion of
CC       arms, blindness, severe mental defect, megalocephaly, and death by
CC       18 months on the average.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Belongs to the aspA/astE family. Aspartoacylase
CC       subfamily.
