RA   Velonia K., Tsigos I., Bouriotis V., Smonou I.;
RT   "Stereospecificity of hydrogen transfer by the NAD+-linked alcohol
RT   dehydrogenase from the antarctic psychrophile Moraxella sp. TAE123.";
RL   Bioorg. Med. Chem. Lett. 9:65-68(1999).
CC   -!- FUNCTION: A psychrophilic enzyme that oxidizes mainly primary and
CC       secondary aliphatic alcohols, as well as large bulky alcohols.
CC       Also catalyzes the reduction of ketones and aldehydes but in lower
CC       rates than the oxidations. The enzyme reduces stereospecifically
CC       2-butanone to produce (s)-2-butanol.
CC   -!- CATALYTIC ACTIVITY: An alcohol + NAD(+) = an aldehyde or ketone +
CC       NADH.
CC   -!- COFACTOR: Binds 1 zinc ion per subunit.
CC   -!- ENZYME REGULATION: The presence of Ca(2+) results in a
CC       considerable stabilization of the enzyme.
CC   -!- SUBUNIT: Homotetramer.
CC   -!- MISCELLANEOUS: The optimum pH is 7.5. Highest reaction rate when
CC       ethanol was used as a substrate. Exhibits thermal lability.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Belongs to the zinc-containing alcohol dehydrogenase
CC       family.
