RL   J. Clin. Endocrinol. Metab. 86:4747-4752(2001).
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Probable transcriptional regulator protein that binds to
CC       DNA as dimer and tetramer, but not as a monomer. Binds to G-
CC       doublets in an A/T-rich environment; the preferred motif is a
CC       tandem repeat of ATTGGTTA combined with a TTATTA-box. May be
CC       involved in immune regulation.
CC   -!- SUBUNIT: Homodimer and homotetramer. Interacts with CREBBP.
CC   -!- SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Nuclear and cytoplasmic; associated with
CC       tubular structures and in discrete nuclear dots ressembling ND10
CC       nuclear bodies. May shuttle between nucleus and cytoplasm.
CC   -!- ALTERNATIVE PRODUCTS:
CC       Event=Alternative splicing; Named isoforms=3;
CC         Comment=Additional isoforms seem to exist. Experimental
CC         confirmation may be lacking for some isoforms;
CC       Name=1; Synonyms=AIRE-1;
CC         IsoId=O43918-1; Sequence=Displayed;
CC       Name=2; Synonyms=AIRE-2;
CC         IsoId=O43918-2; Sequence=VSP_004089;
CC       Name=3; Synonyms=AIRE-3;
CC         IsoId=O43918-3; Sequence=VSP_004089, VSP_004090;
CC   -!- TISSUE SPECIFICITY: Widely expressed. Expressed at higher level in
CC       thymus (medullary epithelial cells and monocyte-dendritic cells),
CC       pancreas, adrenal cortex, and testis. Expressed at lower level in
CC       the spleen, fetal liver and lymph nodes. Isoforms 2 and 3 seem to
CC       be less frequently expressed than isoform 1, if at all.
CC   -!- DOMAIN: The L-X-X-L-L repeats may be implicated in binding to
CC       nuclear receptors.
CC   -!- DOMAIN: The HSR domain is required for localization on tubular
CC       structures (N-terminal part) and for homodimerization.
CC   -!- DOMAIN: Disruption of the first PHD domain has been shown to lead
CC       to reduced transcriptional activity and to localization of the
CC       protein mainly in the cytoplasm in small granules.
CC   -!- PTM: Phosphorylated. Phosphorylation could trigger
CC       oligomerization.
CC   -!- DISEASE: Defects in AIRE are a cause of autoimmune poly-
CC       endocrinopathy candidiasis ectodermal dystrophy (APECED)
CC       [MIM:240300]; also known as autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type
CC       I (APS-1). APECED is an autosomal recessive disease characterized
CC       by: (1) autoimmune polyendocrinopathies: hypoparathyroidism,
CC       adrenocortical failure, IDDM, gonadal failure, hypothyroidism,
CC       pernicious anemia, and hepatitis; (2) chronic mucocutaneous
CC       candidiasis; (3) ectodermal dystrophies: vitiligo, alopecia,
CC       keratopathy, dystrophy of dental enamel, nails and tympanic
CC       membranes. In addition, a high proportion of patients develop
CC       squamous cell carcinoma of the oral mucosa. The disease is
CC       reported worldwide but is exceptionally prevalent among the
CC       Finnish population (incidence 1:25000) and the Iranian jews
CC       (incidence 1:9000).
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Contains 1 HSR domain.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Contains 2 PHD-type zinc fingers.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Contains 1 SAND domain.
CC   -!- DATABASE: NAME=AIRE; NOTE=Article on AIRE and APECED;
CC       WWW="http://chr21.molgen.mpg.de/projects/APECED.html".
