RL   J. Biol. Chem. 278:9052-9057(2003).
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Catalyzes the hydrolysis of short-chain aliphatic amides
CC       to their corresponding organic acids with release of ammonia.
CC       Hydrolyzes propionamide, acetamide and acrylamide, but has no
CC       activity with formamide or urea. The natural substrates of amiE in
CC       its gastric environment are not known. Probably functions to
CC       ensure nitrogen supply to the bacteria.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Also exhibits in vitro acyl transferase activity,
CC       transferring the acyl moiety of short-chain amides to
CC       hydroxylamine to form hydroxamates. The highest level of acyl
CC       transfer activity is observed with acetamide.
CC   -!- CATALYTIC ACTIVITY: A monocarboxylic acid amide + H(2)O = a
CC       monocarboxylate + NH(3).
CC   -!- ENZYME REGULATION: Inhibited by iodoacetate.
CC   -!- SUBUNIT: Homotetramer.
CC   -!- INDUCTION: Repressed by iron via the binding of the fur protein to
CC       the amiE promoter.
CC   -!- MISCELLANEOUS: Optimum temperature for activity is 55 degrees
CC       Celsius and optimum pH is 7.
CC   -!- MISCELLANEOUS: Asp-167 is probably not involved in the catalytic
CC       mechanism, but is probably involved instead in maintenance of the
CC       structural integrity of the amidase.
CC   -!- MISCELLANEOUS: Expression of the amiE gene is stimulated in a
CC       mutant deficient in urease activity, suggesting that production of
CC       this enzyme is regulated to maintain intracellular nitrogen
CC       balance in H.pylori.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Belongs to the nitrilase superfamily. Aliphatic
CC       amidase family.
CC   -!- SIMILARITY: Contains 1 CN hydrolase domain.
